#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084071} :activity/id #uuid "075e6353-42e9-5465-b0d4-8a611aacb2c3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115 :topic/id #uuid "80368ff1-7296-5f6c-aa32-63547e29e578" :topic/math-academy-id 758} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084525 :tutorial/content "An **improper integral** is an integral where at least one limit of integration is unbounded. \n\nFor example, the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nis an improper integral because the upper limit is unbounded (i.e., is infinite).\n\nThis improper integral can be interpreted as the shaded area under the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ shown below. \n\n![](images/38/387754335eb4fe9cc1da3994c74d184fb7a76f44d06e7cdd843ecce043fc790f.png)\n\nTo compute an improper integral, we need to compute the limit of the integral as the upper limit approaches infinity. So, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{a\\to\\infty} \\int_2^a \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo compute this improper integral, we start by integrating as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }([-\\frac{1}{a}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the limits as $a\\to \\infty{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{a}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac12.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "07bb29f4-5642-57f0-814e-1aa4cd26e1e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 927 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c285e20d-e2ec-53fd-8f92-b4af1eb89c5a" :step/math-academy-id 1815 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084072}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083195 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083001 :question/id #uuid "14b4c34b-bbb0-5a1a-b987-b2a60f0e9fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480728} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370627}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit"} :step/id #uuid "a81991d4-09b6-50b8-916a-56e523699675" :step/math-academy-id 1816 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083002 :question/id #uuid "efc8fbd2-c814-5c0b-b781-81829ffda2e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370633}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit"} :step/id #uuid "733cda70-34ab-5a5f-b160-4a78fdfc6d84" :step/math-academy-id 1819 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084527 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, calculating an improper integral involves taking a limit. \n\nWhen computing this limit, we typically have two possible cases:\n\n- If the limit exists, as it has in the previous examples, we say that the integral is **convergent**.\n\n- However, if the limit does not exist or is infinite, we say that the integral is **divergent**.\n\nLet's discuss a concrete example of a divergent improper integral. Consider the following:\n\n$$\nI = \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to work out $I$ by integrating over $x\\in[1,a]$ as usual and taking the limit as $a$ tends to positive infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}|x|]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }([\\operatorname{ln}a]-[\\operatorname{ln}1]) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }([\\operatorname{ln}a]-[0]) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}a) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit of $I$ as $a\\rightarrow \\infty$ is infinite. Therefore, the integral $I$ is *divergent*." :tutorial/id #uuid "7dfef442-a6e1-5446-ac2a-c51aafd1f10c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 930 :tutorial/title "Divergent Improper Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "5b91809e-4ecc-558a-907a-e1c63422b0b8" :step/math-academy-id 1822 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084085}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083201 :k